            <title><![CDATA[The US Government Is Hiding Secret Court Ruling About How It Spies on Americans (150 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.wired.com/story/the-us-government-is-hiding-a-secret-court-ruling-about-how-it-spies-on-americans/</link>
            <guid>50259604</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:06:42 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/the-us-government-is-hiding-a-secret-court-ruling-about-how-it-spies-on-americans/">https://www.wired.com/story/the-us-government-is-hiding-a-secret-court-ruling-about-how-it-spies-on-americans/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50259604">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-testid="ArticlePageChunks"><div data-journey-hook="grid-wrapper" data-testid="BodyWrapper"><figure data-testid="cne-audio-embed-figure"></figure><div><p><span>Two Democratic senators</span> who have long overseen <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/the-fbi-doubles-down-on-easing-bestiality-hiring-standards/">US intelligence</a> say the Trump administration is illegally withholding a secret court’s findings from Congress until lawmakers renew a program that lets federal agents read Americans’ communications without a warrant.</p><p>Seven months ago, the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court objected to search filters that let intelligence analysts pull Americans’ messages from intercepted data without the approvals, written justifications, or records the law requires. The court approved another year of surveillance anyway. The ruling has never been made public, and Senators Ron Wyden of Oregon and Adam Schiff of California say the administration’s own deadline for releasing it passed in September.</p><p>The program, Section 702 of the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act (FISA), lapsed in June after Congress failed to renew it, though collection continues under the court’s March approval. The administration’s <a data-offer-url="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/702_Post_RISAA-FINAL.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/702_Post_RISAA-FINAL.pdf&quot;}" href="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/702_Post_RISAA-FINAL.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">own public documentation</a> says rulings like this one are reviewed for public release “within 180 days of issuance.” That window closed September 13, even as intelligence officials press lawmakers to reauthorize the program by the end of the year.</p><p>Wyden, the longest-serving member of the Senate Intelligence Committee, and Schiff, the former chair of its House counterpart, say the administration owes Congress an explanation for the delay, and the names of the officials responsible, by November 6.</p><p>“If this administration won’t follow this simple statutory directive,” the senators told Director of National Intelligence Jay Clayton and Attorney General Todd Blanche in a <a data-offer-url="https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/28833648-2026-10-26-letter-from-senators-wyden-and-schiff-to-dni-clayton-and-ag-blanche/" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/28833648-2026-10-26-letter-from-senators-wyden-and-schiff-to-dni-clayton-and-ag-blanche/&quot;}" href="https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/28833648-2026-10-26-letter-from-senators-wyden-and-schiff-to-dni-clayton-and-ag-blanche/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">demand letter obtained exclusively by WIRED</a>, “it is unclear why Congress or the American people should trust this administration to follow any of the other legal limits to Section 702.”</p><p>Outside Congress, Demand Progress and 22 other civil liberties and government accountability groups on Monday <a data-offer-url="https://www.demandprogress.org/api/media/file/FISCDeadline.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.demandprogress.org/api/media/file/FISCDeadline.pdf&quot;}" href="https://www.demandprogress.org/api/media/file/FISCDeadline.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">urged congressional leaders to refuse to reauthorize Section 702</a> “while the administration actively withholds legally mandated information about how the surveillance program operates.” Signatories range from the American Civil Liberties Union to the conservative-backed Project for Privacy and Surveillance Accountability.</p><p>The Trump administration has already been pressed, and has promised, to release the ruling. In April, senators Tom Cotton and Mark Warner, the Intelligence Committee’s Republican chair and Democratic vice chair, <a href="https://www.congress.gov/119/crec/2026/04/30/172/76/CREC-2026-04-30-pt1-PgS2158-2.pdf">pressed the administration</a> to release it within 15 days so it could inform the Senate’s debate. In July, <a href="https://www.judiciary.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/98781d53-bdcc-75d8-c507-1b1bca492142/2026-07-15_QFR-Responses_Blanche_687007f1-363d-40d9-b7da-356b52dc448d.pdf">asked in writing</a> whether he would commit to declassifying it by the 180-day deadline, Blanche replied, “Yes.”</p><p>Warner “has been clear that he supports transparency with regard to Section 702,” his communications director, Rachel Cohen, tells WIRED. Neither former director of national intelligence Tulsi Gabbard nor Bill Pulte, who briefly replaced her as acting director, “were responsive,” Cohen says, “but Senator Warner expects that DNI Clayton will declassify the opinion, consistent with the bipartisan letter and as required by law.”</p></div></div><div data-journey-hook="grid-wrapper" data-testid="BodyWrapper"><p>Cotton’s office did not respond to a request for comment. The White House, the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, and the US Justice Department did not answer questions WIRED sent Friday about the delay.</p><p>Clayton, who is legally responsible as director of national intelligence for declassifying the ruling, has made no such promise. Asked before his July confirmation whether he would ensure the opinion was public before Congress voted on reauthorization, <a href="https://www.intelligence.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/aphq-clayton-071526.pdf">he wrote only</a> that the government was “working arduously to expeditiously declassify” it and had made the full opinion available to lawmakers.</p><p>Some of what the court found is already known. A FISA court opinion <a data-offer-url="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/declassified/2025/FISC_Opinion_Cert_ABC_03182025_Redacted.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/declassified/2025/FISC_Opinion_Cert_ABC_03182025_Redacted.pdf&quot;}" href="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/declassified/2025/FISC_Opinion_Cert_ABC_03182025_Redacted.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">declassified last year</a> described a similar FBI tool, a “participants filter” that let agents select people in contact with surveillance targets and read their messages. In February 2025, the Justice Department’s National Security Division told the court the tool had been running searches no one counted. The FBI shut it off the next month and <a href="https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/reports/26-002_0.pdf">switched it back on later that year</a>, after the department approved a rebuilt version.</p><p>This year’s ruling went beyond that. The court found that tools agencies use to narrow intercepted data to an American’s messages amount to searches of Americans, subject to the same limits, and ordered them rebuilt, The New York Times <a data-offer-url="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/09/us/politics/section-702-surveillance-fisa.html" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/09/us/politics/section-702-surveillance-fisa.html&quot;}" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/09/us/politics/section-702-surveillance-fisa.html" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">reported in April</a>, citing unclassified administration talking points sent to lawmakers. Senator Alex Padilla said in a written question to Blanche that the Justice Department “is appealing portions” of the ruling “limiting the government’s use of certain filtering tools.”</p><p>While the ruling stayed secret, <a data-offer-url="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/FISA_Section_702_By_The_Numbers-FINAL.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/FISA_Section_702_By_The_Numbers-FINAL.pdf&quot;}" href="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/FISA_Section_702_By_The_Numbers-FINAL.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">seven intelligence agency fact sheets</a> from March 2026, one <a data-offer-url="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/US_Person_Queries_FISA_Section_702-FINAL.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/US_Person_Queries_FISA_Section_702-FINAL.pdf&quot;}" href="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/US_Person_Queries_FISA_Section_702-FINAL.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">dated the day of the ruling</a>, cited a 98.9 percent compliance rate for FBI searches of Americans and credited Section 702 with stopping a planned terrorist attack <a data-offer-url="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/CIA_FISA_Factsheet_FINAL_20260327.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/CIA_FISA_Factsheet_FINAL_20260327.pdf&quot;}" href="https://www.intelligence.gov/assets/documents/702-documents/CIA_FISA_Factsheet_FINAL_20260327.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">on a Taylor Swift concert in Vienna</a>. None mentions the court’s findings. The figure appears nowhere in the Justice Department inspector general’s <a href="https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/reports/26-002_0.pdf">October 2025 review</a> of those searches, which put the compliance rate at 98.6 percent and could not conclude that “the FBI’s querying compliance issues are entirely in the past.”</p></div><div data-journey-hook="grid-wrapper" data-testid="BodyWrapper"><p>According to the fact sheets, FBI auditors review every such search. FBI director Kash Patel closed the bureau’s Office of Internal Auditing last May and moved its auditors into another division. The bureau’s search counts before 2025 also omit queries run through the participants filter, searches the government’s own privacy watchdog says are “<a data-offer-url="https://documents.pclob.gov/prod/Documents/OversightReport/315fe19c-07f3-4cc6-986a-ff199ce5b616/Unclassified%20PCLOB%20702%20Report%202026.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://documents.pclob.gov/prod/Documents/OversightReport/315fe19c-07f3-4cc6-986a-ff199ce5b616/Unclassified%20PCLOB%20702%20Report%202026.pdf&quot;}" href="https://documents.pclob.gov/prod/Documents/OversightReport/315fe19c-07f3-4cc6-986a-ff199ce5b616/Unclassified%20PCLOB%20702%20Report%202026.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">not possible to track</a>.”</p><p>Lawmakers who have read the ruling hint that it says more. Senator Dick Durbin, the top Democrat on the Judiciary Committee, urged colleagues <a data-offer-url="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2026-06-02/pdf/CREC-2026-06-02.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2026-06-02/pdf/CREC-2026-06-02.pdf&quot;}" href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2026-06-02/pdf/CREC-2026-06-02.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">on the Senate floor in June</a> to read it in a secure room. “I have seen it,” he said. Once they did, he told them, “your opinion of the role of FISA and the rights of Americans will be impacted for sure.”</p><p>Wyden has also warned about a classified interpretation of Section 702 that he says multiple administrations have relied on and refused to declassify. “When it is eventually declassified,” <a data-offer-url="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2026-03-10/pdf/CREC-2026-03-10.pdf" data-event-click="{&quot;element&quot;:&quot;ExternalLink&quot;,&quot;outgoingURL&quot;:&quot;https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2026-03-10/pdf/CREC-2026-03-10.pdf&quot;}" href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2026-03-10/pdf/CREC-2026-03-10.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">he said in March</a>, “the American people are going to be stunned that it took so long and that Congress had been debating this authority with insufficient information.”</p><p>The Trump administration has until November 6 to answer the senators. The court’s current approval of the program expires in March.</p><p>Surveillance reform advocates say the administration may try to avoid that fight by attaching a renewal to the annual defense authorization bill, which Congress usually finalizes in December. In 2023, lawmakers <a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-bill/2670">extended Section 702 for four months</a> through that year’s defense bill after a Senate effort to strip the provision failed. This year’s House and Senate versions of the bill do not yet include it.</p></div></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Software may be getting worse, but it used to be horrible (201 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.bitlog.com/2026/10/27/software-may-be-getting-worse-but-it-used-to-be-horrible/</link>
            <guid>50257548</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:30:35 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.bitlog.com/2026/10/27/software-may-be-getting-worse-but-it-used-to-be-horrible/">https://www.bitlog.com/2026/10/27/software-may-be-getting-worse-but-it-used-to-be-horrible/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50257548">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p><span><span>Software is degrading all around us, for obvious reasons. </span><a href="https://www.bitlog.com/2026/05/19/why-are-we-getting-worse-at-software-engineering/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-description="Opens in a new tab"><span>I've written about this before.</span></a><span> All else equal, if you increase your code production by 10x but you don't reduce your error rate by a factor of 10, then your production app is buggier than it used to be.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>I've seen threads where people also have some nostalgia for the past: "software used to be better in the past" or "we've forgotten how to program like we used to" or "software quality has been declining for decades." And as someone who has used computers since 1990, I'm here to disabuse you of this notion. Software was horrible. Everything crashed all the time. Yes, there were great programmers like John Carmack who could keep the entire program in their head and iterate on thoughtful codebases over decades. No, they did not write a majority of the software that we used to use.</span></span></p>
<h2 id="historical-context"><span><span>Historical context</span></span></h2>
<p><span><span>Almost everyone in the world was on Windows. Windows peaked around a 95% market share, plus or minus a few percentage points based on your source. Yes, you probably knew someone with an iMac who would tell you that it was way better than a Windows machine, and they could prove it if anyone ever actually released applications for Macs. And yes, there were Linux distros like Debian where people could get real computing done</span><span><sup id="footnote-reference-5db95486-37cd-41e6-acc1-941399ea183f"><a href="#footnote-1b731187-c6aa-4eaf-b841-44de9b59b27f" aria-label="Go to footnote 0">[0]</a></sup></span><span>.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>But those were exceptions. Almost every computer user had a Windows machine and either an Intel or an AMD processor. And Windows had a lot of problems.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>There was one saving grace though: companies only got one chance to ship their software. At a certain point, it was going to be loaded onto a floppy disk or burned onto a CD or a ROM, put into a box, and mailed out. And that version had to work out of the box. So there was some quality floor that you couldn't slip below.</span></span></p>
<h2 id="how-bad-did-it-get"><span><span>How bad did it get?</span></span></h2>
<p><span><span>Here's a collection of things that I remember. This isn't linear; it's my collection of memories from 1990 and onward.</span></span></p>
<h3 id="blue-screen-of-death"><span><span>Blue Screen of Death</span></span></h3>
<p><span><span>Windows had extreme stability issues for most of my childhood.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>Let me introduce you to the song of my people, the Blue Screen of Death.</span></span></p>
<figure data-media-id="1a861cd1-64d8-4e42-8f28-ef1a53d6f55a"><p><span><span><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="https://www.bitlog.com/media/fecabf30-3bef-42c9-98d7-337657a2f509/v2/320.avif 320w, https://www.bitlog.com/media/fecabf30-3bef-42c9-98d7-337657a2f509/v2/640.avif 640w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://www.bitlog.com/media/fecabf30-3bef-42c9-98d7-337657a2f509/v2/320.webp 320w, https://www.bitlog.com/media/fecabf30-3bef-42c9-98d7-337657a2f509/v2/640.webp 640w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px"><img src="https://www.bitlog.com/media/fecabf30-3bef-42c9-98d7-337657a2f509/v2/640.jpg" srcset="https://www.bitlog.com/media/fecabf30-3bef-42c9-98d7-337657a2f509/v2/320.jpg 320w, https://www.bitlog.com/media/fecabf30-3bef-42c9-98d7-337657a2f509/v2/640.jpg 640w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px" width="640" height="480" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span></span></p>
<figcaption><p><span><span>The blue screen of death in Windows XP, Vista and 7, </span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_screen_of_death" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-description="Opens in a new tab"><span>courtesy of Wikipedia</span></a></span></p></figcaption></figure>
<p><span><span>This was Windows' version of a kernel panic. It happened right when you were in the middle of something. Or maybe you were doing nothing. It didn't matter: the blue screen was coming.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>Your computer crashed for a lot of reasons. First, device drivers. If you bought some new peripheral like a joystick, it'd come with a floppy disk or CD with device drivers. You'd dutifully install them. The device drivers had the same function as they do today: they help the operating system understand how to interface with the hardware. This might get you thinking, "Oh wow, so they sent out the device drivers on physical media? You can't update a physical disk once you ship it. Surely they took extra care to get everything right." My friend, this could not be further from the truth. I'm sure there were good drivers and bad drivers. But in aggregate, they were buggy, and it only took one marginal device driver to destabilize your computer.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>The Windows operating system also had its own code quality issues back then, and would sometimes fail to fully reclaim resources from programs that exited. So your computer would slowly degrade over time until eventually the slow rot would take down your machine.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>All in all, your computer would just blink out of existence from time to time. It was a fact of life.</span></span></p>
<h3 id="autosave"><span><span>Autosave</span></span></h3>
<p><span><span>Do you know what pairs really well with a computer that crashes all the time? Applications that don't autosave. Even better, applications with autosave that don't flush buffered writes and corrupt their save files.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>It was commonplace to get into the zone and work on a document for hours, and then suddenly you would get either a Blue Screen of Death, or a pop-up saying that your application had a problem and had to exit. And you'd scream and you'd double-click your document over and over, but it was no use. You had to start over. But there was a consolation prize. It was your turn to tell everyone, "I was working on a document for THREE HOURS! And then the computer crashed and I lost everything. It was all gone. I had to redo it." There was always that one asshole that was like "why didn't you save?" But you didn't get mad. You knew that he was 2 weeks from having the same thing happen to him.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>When I joined the Google Docs team back in 2010, we instrumented the Ctrl+s keyboard shortcut for fun. It didn't do anything but record a metric saying that it had been pressed. This "save" keyboard shortcut was in the top 5 non-editing user actions, just behind things like "move the cursor with arrow keys." And it did absolutely nothing. Google Docs autosaves. But everyone was so traumatized from years or decades of losing work that they reflexively saved anytime they edited anything.</span></span></p>
<h3 id="nothing-was-secure"><span><span>Nothing was secure</span></span></h3>
<p><span><span>When my sister and I were little, we loved going into stores with display units. The computers would often have games installed you could play. Worst case, you could always open up MS Paint or Solitaire and mess around for a few minutes.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>One time, we were in a store where the display models had password-protected screensavers. My sister was undeterred. She just hit Ctrl+Alt+Del, selected the screensaver application, and clicked "End Task." And we were in. The best minds of Microsoft were nothing next to the hacking talent of a 9-year-old.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>And this is the level of security that your computer afforded you. If you used the Internet for long enough, your computer would start to collect a bunch of advertising malware. When you turned your computer on, you would start to see pop-ups and pop-unders with random ads, even when you weren't using browsers. So you'd do another antivirus scan, or later on you could visit websites that could also run the malware scans for you.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>And not to mention the viruses that would </span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melissa_(computer_virus)" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-description="Opens in a new tab"><span>drop the Internet to its knees</span></a><span> once a year by doing trivial things like "adding a macro to a Word document that sends emails."</span></span></p>
<h3 id="disk-fragmentation"><span><span>Disk fragmentation</span></span></h3>
<p><span><span>Any techie can remember going to a nontechnical relative's house. In some dusty corner, they'd have an ancient Windows computer barely hanging on by a thread. It would literally take 20 minutes to turn on.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>You'd sigh and say, "let me fix this for you."</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>You would open two programs. This would take about 5 minutes. The first program emptied cache files and the Recycle Bin. The second was Disk Defragmenter.</span></span></p>
<figure data-media-id="fabdb695-71f2-423e-b3d7-d967d5ecb843"><p><span><span><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="https://www.bitlog.com/media/3f726b82-fe15-4153-8c10-952bf2723356/v2/320.avif 320w, https://www.bitlog.com/media/3f726b82-fe15-4153-8c10-952bf2723356/v2/638.avif 638w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://www.bitlog.com/media/3f726b82-fe15-4153-8c10-952bf2723356/v2/320.webp 320w, https://www.bitlog.com/media/3f726b82-fe15-4153-8c10-952bf2723356/v2/638.webp 638w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px"><img src="https://www.bitlog.com/media/3f726b82-fe15-4153-8c10-952bf2723356/v2/638.jpg" srcset="https://www.bitlog.com/media/3f726b82-fe15-4153-8c10-952bf2723356/v2/320.jpg 320w, https://www.bitlog.com/media/3f726b82-fe15-4153-8c10-952bf2723356/v2/638.jpg 638w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px" width="638" height="469" alt="A screenshot of disk defragmenter" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span></span></p>
<figcaption><p><span><span>Your computer slowly became unusable if you never saw this window.</span></span></p></figcaption></figure>
<p><span><span>Back in the day, hard drives in Wintel boxes stored data with spinning magnetic platters. The hard drive had a retractable arm with a little magnetic reader, and it would read data by retracting the arm to the right position and then moving the disk to the start of a sector.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>But of course, as you add more and more files, the game of Tetris doesn't work, and the rest of your file doesn't neatly fit onto the disk before the next file's record begins. So your file would end up in two (or more) places on the hard drive. This was called fragmentation. A little bit of fragmentation was unavoidable, but if more and more files became fragmented then your computer became slower and slower until eventually Grandpa's computer took 20 minutes to start.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>Many modern filesystems, Windows' included, automatically handle defragmentation in the background nowadays. But Windows did not back then. You needed to periodically manually start the defragmentation process yourself.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>By the time I got into college, I discovered that Windows had a scheduler and could run Disk Defragmenter whenever I wanted, so I just left my computer on all the time and let it defragment at 5 in the morning.</span></span></p>
<h3 id="phenomenal-cosmic-power-itty-bitty-memory"><span><span>Phenomenal cosmic power, itty bitty memory</span></span></h3>
<p><span><span>Applications were heavily memory constrained, so programmers tried to save bits and bytes everywhere.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>This should have been invisible to me, but it wasn't.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>At one point I had a crappy video game version of Axis and Allies for the PC, released in 1998</span><span><sup id="footnote-reference-96122f4e-306d-409e-95b2-d08d7c1e23be"><a href="#footnote-0026d375-ee47-418b-9329-0da2c5fd8cc3" aria-label="Go to footnote 1">[1]</a></sup></span><span>. Imagine Risk but World War 2. And the AI was really bad, and could generally be beaten easily. But the game had a major flaw: If a country had more than 255 units, the whole game would crash. And naturally the AI also loved to turtle, so it was common for the AI to have a territory with over a hundred units that was impossible to wear down. So because of this programming choice, the game was a race to wipe out the enemy before it could force a stalemate by crashing the game.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>This kind of thing happened all the time. During my first programming class in my freshman year of high school, one of our lessons was effectively, "here's why you all recognize numbers like 128, 256, and maybe 65536."</span></span></p>
<h3 id="software-came-on-unreliable-physical-media"><span><span>Software came on unreliable physical media</span></span></h3>
<p><span><span>Even if the software worked perfectly, you might scratch your game CD and lose the ability to play it. Then you'd start asking your friends if anyone had a spare copy of Red Alert or something, just so that you could finally finish the single-player campaign.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>This wasn't unique to CDs. Anything physical could degrade. My half-brother had a NES game called Dragon Warrior or something, but the cartridge was corrupted and there was no way to leave the first room. And even if you kept all of your games in good condition, the contacts inside of the NES that connected the cartridge to the console could rust and fail. This kind of thing happened all the time.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>"But Jake, that's hardware and not software." All I'm saying is that y</span><span>our software was fragile and it needed to be protected and preserved.</span><span> It's not like you had the option for a physical disk or the online install and you chose the disk. By the nature of distribution, the software itself would degrade and rot away from you.</span></span></p>
<h3 id="online-games-assumed-good-behavior"><span><span>Online games assumed good behavior</span></span></h3>
<p><span><span>One of the earliest online games I played was Diablo II, which means that I have seen the true heart of darkness inside of humanity.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>Online games have a strange problem: gamers often don't want to be together. They just happen to cohabitate a space. And Diablo II had a lot of design features that lent themselves to chaos:</span></span></p>
<ul data-list-id="db6355e4-2ae8-4dd7-abf4-d78de64ddeac" data-list-depth="0" data-last="false"><li><p><span><span>There were rampant client-side hacks. In PVP servers, people would use hacks to e.g. fire tracking arrows far beyond the range that they should have been able to.</span></span></p></li></ul>
<ul data-list-id="db6355e4-2ae8-4dd7-abf4-d78de64ddeac" data-list-depth="0" data-last="false"><li><p><span><span>All item drops were equally available to everyone in the server. That means when you got close to killing an act boss, you and your party would transition from allies to rivals and spam click when the boss finally popped.</span></span></p></li></ul>
<ul data-list-id="db6355e4-2ae8-4dd7-abf4-d78de64ddeac" data-list-depth="0" data-last="false"><li><p><span><span>If you had a corpse on the ground and died again, you'd lose everything you had equipped. People would try to force you to have a full inventory by dropping a bunch of cheap items onto your corpse, hoping that you'd accidentally pick up common items before picking up your corpse. Then you'd try to pick up your corpse. You'd only pick up some of the items. Then they'd kill you a second time and you'd lose everything that you managed to pick up.</span></span></p></li></ul>
<ul data-list-id="db6355e4-2ae8-4dd7-abf4-d78de64ddeac" data-list-depth="0" data-last="false"><li><p><span><span>The game had a lot of item duping glitches. As you can imagine, this ruined the in-game economy since it became dominated by the duped items. And it enabled a lot of scams. They'd pretend that they just duped an item, and they'd wait for someone to ask how they did it. Then they'd walk the user through step-by-step, but one step would involve dropping your item on the ground and then the scammer would pick up the item.</span></span></p></li></ul>
<ul data-list-id="db6355e4-2ae8-4dd7-abf4-d78de64ddeac" data-list-depth="0" data-last="true"><li><p><span><span>There was no guild system, but people still ran guilds. This means that there was no trusted way to identify guaranteed friendlies.</span></span></p></li></ul>
<p><span><span>Many of these were fixed by the time Diablo III came around. They had extra guards against duplicated items. Bosses gave players individual drops, so there was no competition for items when you killed bosses. And you didn't need to recover items from your corpse in a way that risked you losing items. These were all hard-won lessons.</span></span></p>
<h3 id="turbo-button"><span><span>Turbo button</span></span></h3>
<p><span><span>I started using my family's computer around 1990 when I learned how to read. The computer was a beige 286 that sat on our desk with the monitor perched on top. It had a 5.25" floppy drive. It had a power button. It had a reset button. And it had a TURBO button. The TURBO button was supposed to be ignored most of the time, but you could press it when you needed to slow the computer's processor down.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>Why would you need to do that? Because IBM's first desktop computers ran on the 8088 processor at 4.77MHz</span><span><sup id="footnote-reference-1c1ff7a7-fe1d-4edc-b26c-a30a728acf7e"><a href="#footnote-d933dcdf-919d-4e8e-ac47-6b598d77eb8f" aria-label="Go to footnote 2">[2]</a></sup></span><span>. It didn't stay this way for long. But there was a problem: many programmers wrote their applications with the assumption that computers had that 4.77MHz processor. Programmers synchronized animations, video game frame rates, and generally relied on the assumption that they were working with a fixed clock speed. If you used one of these applications on a faster processor, they would likely be unusable.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>Computer manufacturers devised a simple solution: add a TURBO button that would just slow your CPU down on demand. Whenever you ran into an application that had timing issues, just press that TURBO button and watch it slow down to a comfortable speed.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>I never needed to actually press the TURBO button in anger. My understanding is that most of the applications that benefitted from the TURBO button were games, and we only played newer educational games that didn't have these timing issues. When editing this post, I thought about dropping this section a few times. But can you imagine having an "Original iPhone" button on the side of your iPhone so that the Notes app could work? Good grief.</span></span></p>
<h2 id="modern-software-isnt-perfect"><span><span>Modern software isn't perfect</span></span></h2>
<p><span><span>Modern software isn't perfect. It's bloated. It's slow. It was written by too many people. It's too complex. It has too many cooks in the kitchen. And now, we're losing our grasp on how it works under the hood as we delegate more and more to the agents.</span></span></p>
<p><span><span>But I'd never go back to the way software used to be written. Give me process isolation and online updates. Give me higher-level programming languages that abstract common pitfalls from C and C++. Give me game designers that handle timing using the system clock. Give me Steam and Windows Update. You can keep your old release process that fell by the wayside.</span></span></p>
<section id="footnote-1b731187-c6aa-4eaf-b841-44de9b59b27f" aria-label="Footnote 0"><a href="#footnote-reference-5db95486-37cd-41e6-acc1-941399ea183f" aria-label="Back to reference 0">[0]</a><p><span><span>From what I remember from trying to install Debian in my high school years, the most popular app on Linux was anything that could edit </span><code><span>XF86Config</span></code><span>.</span></span></p>
</section>
<section id="footnote-0026d375-ee47-418b-9329-0da2c5fd8cc3" aria-label="Footnote 1"><a href="#footnote-reference-96122f4e-306d-409e-95b2-d08d7c1e23be" aria-label="Back to reference 1">[1]</a><p><span><span>I'm having trouble corroborating this anecdote with information I can find online. The details might not be exactly right. But this is what happened, to the extent that the memory survived 25ish years.</span></span></p>
</section>
<section id="footnote-d933dcdf-919d-4e8e-ac47-6b598d77eb8f" aria-label="Footnote 2"><a href="#footnote-reference-1c1ff7a7-fe1d-4edc-b26c-a30a728acf7e" aria-label="Back to reference 2">[2]</a><p><span><span>Not GHz, MHz!</span></span></p>
</section>
</div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Python's experimental JIT to be removed (213 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://lwn.net/SubscriberLink/1102102/0c00e4d550f25f8c/</link>
            <guid>50257419</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:17:57 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://lwn.net/SubscriberLink/1102102/0c00e4d550f25f8c/">https://lwn.net/SubscriberLink/1102102/0c00e4d550f25f8c/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50257419">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
                	<summary>[+] Proceed to the article</summary>
                
<p>Python's <a href="https://docs.python.org/3.13/whatsnew/3.13.html#an-experimental-just-in-time-jit-compiler">experimental
just-in-time (JIT) compiler</a>, which was added to CPython in 2024 for the <a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3130/">3.13&nbsp;release</a>,
is on its way out. In June, the Python steering council <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/an-announcement-from-the-steering-council-regarding-the-jit-project/107638">announced</a>
that it was pausing work on the JIT in CPython's main branch until there was an
accepted <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001">Python Enhancement
Proposal</a> (PEP) that addressed concerns about maintenance, compatibility, and
set clear success metrics as well as a timeline for completion. After creating
<a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0836/">PEP&nbsp;836</a> ("JIT Go Brrr: The
Path to a Supported JIT Compiler for CPython")
to try to meet the council's request, and after months of discussion, the
JIT maintainers have withdrawn the PEP. For now, it appears that development on
the once-promising project has come to an end.</p>

<p>The JIT was <a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/113465">added to CPython by Brandt
Bucher</a> in early 2024. Generally, such a major feature would be expected to
land only after the council had approved a <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001/#pep-types:~:text=A%20Standards%20Track%20PEP">standards-track PEP</a>. Bucher had given
a talk (<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HxSHIpEQRjs">YouTube video</a>,
<a href="https://github.com/brandtbucher/brandtbucher/blob/master/2023/10/10/a_jit_compiler_for_cpython.pdf">slides</a>)
about the JIT at a Python core developer sprint, and <a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/113465#issuecomment-1911279536">announced
his intent</a> to merge the feature—but he had not taken the step of
authoring or submitting a PEP for its approval. He was <a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/pull/113465#issuecomment-1969415137">prodded
into doing so</a> by steering council member Thomas Wouters, who was also
serving as the release manager for Python 3.13.</p>

<p>Bucher and Savannah Ostrowski drafted <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0744/">PEP&nbsp;744</a> ("JIT Compilation"),
which was an <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001/#pep-types:~:text=An%20Informational%20PEP%20describes%20a%20Python%20design%20issue">informational
PEP</a>—meaning it only provides information to the Python community and
does not represent formal consensus to proceed on a feature or project. LWN <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/970397/">covered</a> the discussion of
PEP&nbsp;744 in April&nbsp;2024, and later covered Bucher's <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/977855/">talk on the JIT</a> at
PyCon&nbsp;US&nbsp;2024, as well as the <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/1029307/">follow-up talk</a> at
PyCon&nbsp;US&nbsp;2025.</p>

<h4>PEP it up</h4>

<p>Work on the JIT had been proceeding on the basis of PEP&nbsp;744 until this
June, when the council had put forward its formal request for the JIT maintainers to
create a standards-track PEP. One might wonder, <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/an-announcement-from-the-steering-council-regarding-the-jit-project/107638/2">as
Antoine Pitrou did</a>, why the council had decided that it was time to speak up
and ask for a new PEP. Wouters <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/an-announcement-from-the-steering-council-regarding-the-jit-project/107638/4">said</a>
that there was no "<q>single triggering event for this request</q>", but that
the council had been discussing the topic for a while and indicated that it was
looking to ensure the JIT work did not interfere with other projects. "<q>There's been work
trying to get the JIT to play nice with instrumentation, debugging, and
profiling. There's also external JITs (like CinderX), JIT-adjacent things (like
Numba and PyTorch's compile), and discussions about a tier 3 JIT.</q>" The
council had waited until after the Python&nbsp;3.15 beta release "<q>to be clear
that we're OK with the status quo for 3.15</q>".</p>

<p>The council asked for a PEP that would make the case for the JIT "<q>as a supported,
non-experimental part of CPython: its guarantees, its maintenance commitments,
and its impact on redistributors</q>". The council set a deadline of six months
for the PEP to be submitted and accepted; failing that, the council would
require the JIT code to be removed from the CPython main branch.</p>

<!-- middle-ad -->

<p>Bucher, Ostrowski, and Ken Jin drafted PEP&nbsp;836 and <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010">published
it on the Python forum</a> for community feedback on July&nbsp;3. It generated
quite a bit of discussion, some favorable and some critical. One of the key
themes in the comments, such as <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/19">this
one</a> by Wouters, and <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/28">this
comment</a> by Mark Shannon, was that the draft felt like it should be two separate
PEPs. Shannon, for example, thought that the PEP should only
cover goals for accepting the JIT work and felt that it should leave out
implementation details. There were also quite a few calls for additional
information.</p>

<p>Bucher <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/21">pushed
back</a> a bit on the suggestion that it should be two PEPs, and Ostrowski <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/22">defended</a>
the structure of the PEP as it was. "<q>Outlining goals like we have in the PEP
without being able to share the plan and everything we've thought through
alongside it would make me uncomfortable. To me, goals without plans behind them
aren't really goals at all.</q>"</p>

<p>When we <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/1090385/">last looked at this
topic</a> in September, the discussion about the PEP had slowed down without a
commitment to develop new PEPs or a formal statement from the council. The last
word from the council at that time, in the form of a <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/2026-08-06-python-steering-council-meeting-summary/108612">summary
of its August&nbsp;6 meeting</a>, was that it had met with the PEP authors and
was "<q>discussing the state of the PEP and whether it answered the questions
the SC asked in its request for a PEP</q>".</p>

<h4>Experimentation in main</h4>

<p>On September&nbsp;21, Wouters <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/53">posted</a>
a statement from the steering council, minus Ostrowski (who is currently a
member of the steering council but did not participate since she is a co-author
of the PEP), that summarized its position. The statement
said that PEP did answer all of the questions that were highlighted in the
council's request, "<q>although perhaps some less convincingly than
others</q>".</p>

<p>The council wished to make clear that it was not
looking for the criteria that the JIT should meet to be a supported feature, or
whether it should be considered such. Instead, the council was evaluating
whether the JIT should continue as an experiment in CPython's main branch.</p>

<blockquote>
Part of the problem with allowing experimentation in main is the lack of unified
design, and the uncertainty that core developers, package maintainers and users
face as the experimentation changes behaviours and API surfaces.
</blockquote>

<p>The council said that the PEP was not specific enough about support plans, performance
criteria, and too vague or too optimistic about a number of other areas. The
statement included a ten-point list of items that the council wanted "<q>to see
either spelled out in the PEP, or at least discussed and rejected</q>". The
council's list expressed concerns ranging from memory impact to security. For
example, on the topic of platform parity, the council wanted to ensure that the
JIT was designed to be easily adopted for new platforms. It wanted assurance
that the JIT's design would not be tied to specific platform or require
platform-specific knowledge to maintain.</p>

<p>Another area that the council called out was user impact. It said that the
PEP was "<q>overly optimistic</q>" about user-visible differences with the JIT
enabled. There were many cases where users depend on idiosyncrasies in the
interpreter design, "<q>as we've seen with other Python implementations or even
just between major Python versions</q>", and it wanted clarification on how
those differences would be handled. Would they be considered bugs, how would
situations be handled if a difference in behavior was unavoidable, and so
on.</p>

<p>Security was "<q>an underserved area in the PEP</q>", according to the
council; it suggested that a discussion with the <a href="https://devguide.python.org/security/psrt/">Python security response
team</a> was in order and that a security review should be part of the criteria
for supported status as well.</p>

<p>PEP 836 specified a <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0836/#compatibility-review">compatibility
review</a> milestone to correspond with the first Python&nbsp;3.17 release
candidate—which would be expected sometime in August&nbsp;2028—that
would involve running the test suites of the top PyPI packages to see if the JIT
broke them. The council wanted that timeline moved up significantly. "<q>We
think that's way, way too late, and the compatibility review should be an
ongoing and continuous process starting as soon as possible</q>".

</p><p>The council also worried about the JIT's design decisions being made
"<q>without critical review by the larger group of developers ultimately
responsible for [CPython] development, maintenance and support</q>". The bottom
line, it said, was that PEP&nbsp;836 "<q>mostly answers the questions we
asked</q>" with no missing pieces, but it wanted more discussion on those topics
so it could "<q>gauge community support and consensus before we're asked to
pronounce on this PEP</q>".</p>

<p>Jin <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/55">replied</a>
a few days later to thank the council for its detailed feedback and said that the
group would work on its suggestions.</p>

<h4>Withdrawn</h4>

<p>After some consideration, it appears, the PEP's authors have decided not to
continue pursing the council's approval. On October&nbsp;19, Ostrowski (who is
also release manager for Python&nbsp;3.16 and 3.17) <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/57">announced</a>
that the PEP was being withdrawn:</p>

<blockquote>
The Steering Council rightly sets a high bar for a proposal of this scope, and
we don't feel we have the personal bandwidth to carry the current version
forward. We continue to believe in the technical direction outlined in the PEP,
including free-threaded support and a method-based frontend, and remain
optimistic about the JIT's potential in CPython. We'll make the reference
implementation available and link it from the withdrawn PEP so others who are
interested can explore and build on that.
</blockquote>

<p>Barry Scott <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/59">asked</a>
if that meant that work on the JIT was stopped. Brett Cannon <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/61">replied</a>
that the work would be stopped in the main repository, but it would be up to the
community whether the work would continue elsewhere. Dunc Graham <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/62">suggested</a>
that there might be time for an alternative PEP to be submitted before the
council's deadline. Cannon <a href="https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-836-jit-go-brrr-the-path-to-a-supported-jit-compiler-for-cpython/108010/63">said</a>
that was not likely, "<q>as all the people actively driving the JIT were either
authors on that PEP or contributors to it</q>".</p>

<p>With the PEP withdrawn, the JIT will likely be removed from CPython main
fairly soon to ensure that any problems caused by its removal can be addressed
early in the <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0826/">Python&nbsp;3.16
development cycle</a>. It is unclear whether the original drivers of the JIT
work intend to continue working on it at all, or if it will simply be left for others to
pick up if there is enough community interest. I have emailed the PEP authors
for clarification, but have not yet received a reply.</p><br clear="all">
               <br clear="all">
               <blockquote>
<b>Did you like this article?</b>? <a href="https://lwn.net/Promo/slink-d-obnoxious/claim">Subscribe now</a> at the special discounted rate to get a lot more like it.
</blockquote>
<hr>
            
