        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Wikipedia's Cleanup of AI-Generated Content (203 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://spectrum.ieee.org/wikipedia-wikiproject-ai-writing-cleanup</link>
            <guid>50257326</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:08:15 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/wikipedia-wikiproject-ai-writing-cleanup">https://spectrum.ieee.org/wikipedia-wikiproject-ai-writing-cleanup</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50257326">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-headline="How Wikipedia’s Editors Keep the Online Encyclopedia Human-Written"><p>Ellia Lebleu is a founding member of<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_AI_Cleanup" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"> the WikiProject AI Cleanup</a>, a volunteer effort launched in 2023 to find and remove AI-generated material from <a data-linked-post="2674904016" href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/wikipedia-at-25" target="_blank">Wikipedia</a> that breaks the website’s guidelines. When the cleanup began, <a data-linked-post="2677241069" href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/ai-scientist-research-paper-format" target="_blank">AI writing</a> was often betrayed by conspicuous clichés. Today, the project’s volunteers are developing tools to detect subtler forms of machine-generated content and debating AI’s role in the world’s most widely used encyclopedia. </p><p><strong>When did you realize <a href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/tag/wikipedia">Wikipedia</a> had a problem with AI-generated material?</strong></p><p><strong>Ellia Lebleu:</strong> It was around December 2023. A few other editors and I were chatting on Wikipedia’s Discord server, and we had all seen very strange articles with the same odd characteristics. They repeated phrases like “stands as a testament” or “rich cultural heritage.” They sometimes had conclusion sections, or sections called “Why is this important?” That was very typical of <a href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/tag/chatgpt">ChatGPT</a> in 2023 and early 2024 but very odd for Wikipedia articles—and very easy to spot. Then I decided, let’s create an official project on Wikipedia so more people can deal with this.</p><div id="rebelltitem1" data-id="1" data-reload-ads="false" data-is-image="False" data-href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/wikipedia-wikiproject-ai-writing-cleanup/ellia-lebleu" data-basename="ellia-lebleu" data-post-id="2678080371" data-published-at="1791919225" data-use-pagination="False"><h3 data-role="headline">
            
                Ellia Lebleu
            
            
        </h3><p>Ellia Lebleu is a founding member of the WikiProject AI Cleanup. Lebleu is pursuing a Ph.D. in AI at the <a href="https://www.inria.fr/fr/centre-inria-universite-rennes" target="_blank">Inria Center of the University of Rennes</a>, part of France’s national research institute for digital science and technology.</p></div><p><strong>How do editors spot AI-generated writing today?</strong></p><p><strong>Lebleu:</strong> By 2025, the signs of AI writing were becoming subtler and more structural. For example, AI loves the “rule of three,” negative parallelisms, and other structures of speech that aren’t conclusive by themselves but become meaningful as they accumulate. We also look beyond the article. If someone writes five articles of thousands of words each in 5 minutes, there is reason to suspect AI.</p><p><strong>Does the AI problem just appear in the main text, or also in citations?</strong></p><p><strong>Lebleu: </strong>Fake or misleading citations have become a major worry. Sometimes an AI hallucinates a citation entirely, although that’s much rarer today because of retrieval-augmented generation that allows the AI to find sources. More often, it finds a real source but gets its content completely wrong. AI citations that don’t actually support what is being written are extremely commonplace.</p><p><strong>English Wikipedia recently </strong><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Writing_articles_with_large_language_models" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><strong>prohibited editors</strong></a><strong> from using LLMs to generate articles. How much AI-generated content is still getting through?</strong></p><p><strong>Lebleu:</strong> I’ve been working with [AI-detection company]<a href="https://www.pangram.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"> Pangram</a> to estimate the proportion of AI-generated content. I tested it on Wikipedia content from before ChatGPT and found no false positives, so its results provide a very conservative lower bound. In late 2025, it flagged perhaps 20 percent of new articles and drafts as AI-generated. In early 2026, that went down to 15 or 16 percent. But we don’t yet know whether that’s because our recent policies have had an effect or because models are getting more powerful and Pangram hasn’t had time to adapt.</p><p><strong>Is Wikipedia using AI to combat AI?</strong></p><p><strong>Lebleu:</strong> Another Wikipedia editor has made an AI source verifier, which goes through the sources on a page and checks whether they match the claims. Since it is itself an AI model, there is no guarantee that it will be correct every time. But if you want something that works at scale and filters out obvious cases, it’s a good first step. The Wikimedia Foundation is also working on a tool called <a href="https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Edit_check/Paste_Check" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Paste Check</a>, which can flag text pasted directly from some AI <a href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/tag/chatbots">chatbots</a>.</p><p>And we’ve seen Claude sometimes say, “I can’t write a Wikipedia article; it would go against Wikipedia’s policies.” If all major AI companies implemented that kind of prompt refusal, it wouldn’t solve the problem entirely, because there are still open-weight models that can be tweaked to avoid restrictions. But it would help with people who use commercial models and aren’t especially familiar with the technology. </p><p><strong>Can you tell if the rule prohibiting AI writing is working?</strong></p><p><strong>Lebleu: </strong>It’s still a little early to tell. We see possible effects, such as prompt refusal and the apparent slight drop in AI-generated content. But again, we don’t yet know if that is because models are getting better or because beginners are learning the rules. The vast majority of AI-generated content is submitted by beginner editors, so we’ve made many efforts to make the rule very visible in welcome messages, editing help, and advice. </p><p><strong>What if AI becomes capable of writing truly reliable Wikipedia articles?</strong></p><p><strong>Lebleu:</strong> That’s one of the big questions we’re facing. When the guideline was passed, it was for very practical reasons: We were flooded with AI content, we didn’t have good tools, and we needed to put a stop to it. But since then, it has taken on a more philosophical dimension. Many people have called Wikipedia “the last good place on the internet.” Being a place that was, in theory, free from AI slop has become part of Wikipedia’s identity.</p><p>A lot of Wikipedia’s communication is now centered on the idea that we’re a human-written encyclopedia. Even AI companies find that interesting. It can be valuable to have a purely human-written corpus on which to [train] AI systems. The fact that Wikipedia is still that, and will remain that for at least the near future, makes it a precious resource that isn’t replicated anywhere else.</p><p>But will the people who read and contribute to Wikipedia want it to become partially AI-generated if that makes it more comprehensive? We could preserve Wikipedia’s identity as a human-written corpus while also offering an AI-assisted option.</p><p><strong>Has Wikipedia reckoned with the idea of “Google Zero”—a possible future in which websites no longer get traffic from <a href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/tag/google">Google</a> search?</strong></p><p><strong>Lebleu:</strong> If everything goes through AI search and websites plummet to zero visibility, how do we expose Wikipedia and show that it can be edited and that people can still contribute? Wikipedia is frequently cited in [Google’s] AI Overviews, but users may only see an AI answer citing Wikipedia. They may not realize that Wikipedia is a living project they can edit and contribute to.</p><p>The Wikimedia Foundation is working on an attribution framework that could give AI users more information about the article [being cited]: when it was last edited, how comprehensive it is, whether editors have flagged problems, and perhaps even a call to action. In the future, readers might even contribute through <a href="https://spectrum.ieee.org/tag/ai-models">AI models</a>: “The AI cited Wikipedia, but there seems to be an error. Can I make an edit request and add this other source I found?”</p><p>I can see a very strong symbiotic relationship between Wikipedia and AI models in the future. I don’t see Wikipedia disappearing, but I can see it taking a very different shape. Yesterday, AI was something to clean up. Today, it’s a tool to help us with the cleanup. Tomorrow, it will be the air we breathe.</p><p><em>A condensed version of this article appears in the November 2026 print issue as “Ellia Lebleu.”</em><br></p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[AI promised less work. I've never worked more (414 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://oioannou.com/blog/ai-promised-less-work/</link>
            <guid>50257325</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:08:14 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://oioannou.com/blog/ai-promised-less-work/">https://oioannou.com/blog/ai-promised-less-work/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50257325">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p>The pitch was that AI would give us our time back. Write less boilerplate, delegate the boring parts, go home earlier.</p>
<p>I am not complaining about the tools. I use agents for all of my engineering work now, and I wrote about <a href="https://oioannou.com/blog/ai-engineering-setup-15-months-later/">how that setup evolved</a> and <a href="https://oioannou.com/blog/i-still-read-the-code/">why I still read the code</a>. I can investigate and ship things that would not have been worth starting two years ago.</p>
<p>But somewhere this year I noticed that I am working more than I ever have, and that it feels heavier than it used to. Not because any single task is harder. Because there are so many more of them, all at once.</p>
<p>This post is me trying to understand that, with some data from my own GitHub history.</p>
<h2 id="the-old-load-and-the-new-load">The old load and the new load</h2>
<p>As a full-stack engineer, a normal week for me meant two or three serious tasks. A feature, a tricky bug, maybe a migration. Each one needed real focus, but I could hold one of them in my head at a time, finish it, and move to the next.</p>
<p>Today my week looks completely different. I have several agents running in parallel: one implementing a change, one investigating a production question, one working through a review, another doing research I will need later. None of them needs me all the time. Every one of them needs me <em>sometimes</em>.</p>
<p>That “sometimes” is the problem.</p>
<p>Each agent eventually comes back with a question, a plan to approve, a diff to read or a result to make sense of. Responding means switching contexts: reloading a different problem, a different repository and a different set of decisions.</p>
<p>Two or three serious tasks a week was tiring in a familiar way. Working with many agents is tiring in a new way. My attention is spread across more problems, and I find moving between them much more mentally draining.</p>
<h2 id="what-my-github-history-shows">What my GitHub history shows</h2>
<p>I pulled my GitHub contribution calendar for every month from January 2019 to today and marked my roles at <a href="https://bold.org/">Bold.org</a>, which I joined in January 2020: Full Stack Engineer until August 2022, Principal Engineer until August 2025, and Vice President of Technology since then. These are GitHub’s contribution counts, including private work, so they mix commits, pull requests, reviews and issues. It is a rough signal of activity, not a measure of value. 2019 is an incomplete baseline: my job before Bold.org used a self-hosted GitLab, so GitHub only shows what I did outside it. But the shape is hard to ignore.</p>
<p><img src="https://oioannou.com/img/ai-working-more/contributions-per-month.svg" alt="Monthly GitHub contributions from January 2019 to October 2026, shaded by role, with a second dashed line for public contributions only. 2019, before Bold.org, is incomplete because that job used a self-hosted GitLab. As a Full Stack Engineer, months mostly sit between 130 and 320, peaking at 319 in April 2020. As a Principal Engineer, they mostly sit between 55 and 200. As VP of Technology, 2025 stays around 50 to 150, then 2026 jumps to 277 in January, drops to 77 in June and peaks at 427 in August. Public contributions stay near zero from 2021 to 2025 and reach 68 in April 2026."></p>
<p>The first part of the curve is what I expected:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>My busiest coding years at Bold.org were the early ones.</strong> 2020 was my highest year, with 2,711 contributions. 2021 dropped to 1,839 and 2022 to 1,498.</li>
<li><strong>The Principal Engineer years were flat and lower.</strong> From August 2022 to July 2025 I averaged 114 contributions a month, against 173 as a Full Stack Engineer. My lowest year was 2023, with 1,189.</li>
<li><strong>The first months as VP of Technology looked the same.</strong> From August to December 2025 I averaged 111 a month, and December 2025 was my quietest month since joining Bold.org, with 50.</li>
</ul>
<p>As a Principal Engineer, more of my work went into architecture, deep dives into concepts, setting up projects and planning, rather than fixing bugs or iterating on features. Becoming VP of Technology continued that shift, with even less feature work expected. At least until AI came along.</p>
<p>Then 2026 happened:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>I am back at my Full Stack Engineer pace.</strong> By October 27 I am at 2,102 contributions, already more than any full year from 2021 to 2025. Over the same period in 2020 I had 2,371, so 2026 is close to my busiest year but not above it.</li>
<li><strong>August 2026 is the busiest month in the whole timeline.</strong> 427 contributions, against a previous high of 319 in April 2020.</li>
<li><strong>My normal day moved back up.</strong> The median weekday was 3 or 4 contributions every year from 2022 to 2025. In 2026 it is 7, close to the 8 I had in 2020.</li>
<li><strong>Fewer weekdays without GitHub activity.</strong> Through October 27, I had 18 weekdays with zero contributions, compared with 40–46 across each full year from 2022 to 2025. These are days without recorded GitHub activity, not days off work.</li>
<li><strong>Heavy days became routine again.</strong> From 2022 to 2025 I had exactly one day per year with 24 or more contributions. In 2026 I already have 16, almost the 17 I had in all of 2020.</li>
</ul>
<p>Almost all of this is private work. The dashed line in the chart is public contributions only: it was near zero from 2021 to 2025 and came back this year, with 191 so far, but that is still less than a tenth of 2026.</p>
<p>The twist is the role. In 2020 I was a full-stack engineer doing feature work full time, and this is roughly what that looked like on GitHub. Today I lead engineering at Bold.org and do far less feature work than I used to. On paper my graph should look like my Principal Engineer years, or lower. Instead it is back where it was when writing code was my whole job.</p>
<p>What changed is how much work feels worth taking on. When coding is cheap, I can fix bugs I would previously have left alone, improve patterns across the codebase, take on new projects and turn more ideas into something real. Much of this is work I would never have considered before. Agents did not just make my existing workload faster to finish. They made it easy to give myself a much bigger one.</p>
<h2 id="bursts-then-burnout">Bursts, then burnout</h2>
<p>Looking at the monthly totals, 2026 is not one steady climb. It is a series of bursts.</p>
<p><strong>January and February</strong> were the first clear burst, with 277 and 233 contributions. This is when I started working seriously with agents, and when the observability experiment that became <a href="https://oioannou.com/blog/internal-company-agent-lessons/">our internal company agent</a> took off. Every week revealed something new I could suddenly delegate, and I wanted to try all of it.</p>
<p><strong>March to June</strong> generally winds down, from 203 contributions in March to 77 in June. But <strong>April</strong> was a smaller burst along the way, another month when I had ideas and acted on them. I think improvements in the models were part of that renewed energy. Its monthly total of 187 is below January to March, but it includes a 29-contribution day and 68 public contributions, my highest public total of the year.</p>
<p><strong>August</strong> is the biggest burst of all. Coming back from my summer break, the calendar shows a full week of zero contributions in early August. Then every weekday from August 10 to August 27 was between 14 and 43. August ended at 427 contributions, the busiest month in the whole timeline. September stayed high at 301.</p>
<p>Every cycle starts the same way. A new model or a new workflow makes something possible that was not possible last month, and my head fills with ideas. Because implementation is now cheap, there is no natural friction stopping me from pursuing all of them at once. So I do. I start more agents, open more threads and say yes to more experiments.</p>
<p>That works for a few weeks. Then the reviewing and context switching catch up with me, and I pull back to a more stable flow. I stay there until something exciting comes along, or until a holiday recharges me enough that I go straight back into another burst.</p>
<p>I count three of these cycles since January.</p>
<p>At the end of a burst, I feel tired of AI. I do not want to start another agent or think of more ideas. I do my normal work, but I stop chasing the extra projects and possibilities because I do not have the mental bandwidth for them.</p>
<h2 id="the-low-season-is-not-low-anymore">The low season is not low anymore</h2>
<p>The part that surprised me most is not the peaks. It is how much I am doing between them.</p>
<p>March did not feel like the bursts in January, February or August, but I was still doing substantial work. It had 203 contributions, against my Principal Engineer average of 114 a month. I was still acting on ideas, but that level of activity no longer felt like an exceptional push. Even when I think I am taking it easy, there are still agent results to work through, reviews to finish and small things that are now “just a quick prompt”.</p>
<p>June was genuinely low, with 77 contributions, comparable to my quieter Principal Engineer months. Maybe that was when all the tiredness caught up with me. Not every quiet month is higher than before. But outside the bursts, I still find myself taking on more work than I used to. That is what I mean by the low season not feeling low anymore.</p>
<h2 id="where-that-leaves-me">Where that leaves me</h2>
<p>AI made room for work I would never have considered before. I like being able to fix more things and turn more ideas into something real. But the capacity to implement those ideas has grown much faster than my capacity to think about them. Eventually I stop wanting to start another agent and go back to just doing my normal work. I have not figured out a balance. For now, I am noticing that being able to do more keeps turning into doing more. And I am tired.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>A note on how this article was written:</strong> AI helped me pull and analyze my GitHub contribution data, build the chart and draft this article. The experience, decisions and opinions are mine.</p>
</blockquote>
</div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Roblox downloaded 145GB models from us (including adult content) without asking (377 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://open3dlab.com/news/roblox-downloaded-145-gb-of-models-from-our-sites/</link>
            <guid>50256985</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 27 Oct 2026 10:27:50 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://open3dlab.com/news/roblox-downloaded-145-gb-of-models-from-our-sites/">https://open3dlab.com/news/roblox-downloaded-145-gb-of-models-from-our-sites/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50256985">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><p><strong><p>On 15 and 16 October 2026, a crawler that said it belonged to Roblox walked through almost every project on Open3DLab, SFMLab and SmutBase, and proceeded to download complete copies of 1,027 files from 712 projects. 145.5 GB of our creators' work, downloaded for what we assume are AI training purposes.</p></strong></p><div data-diagrams=""><p>This post briefly describes what happened, what mitigations were taken, and what this signals for the community at large. A more detailed writeup outlining what we saw in our logs will be posted at a later time.</p><h2 id="toc_1">What happened</h2><p>On Sunday the 18th of October, we noticed a crawler making a large number of requests to our sites and download infrastructure. The crawler sent the same user agent on every request, <code>trellis2-data-sourcing/1.0 (3D foundation model research; [redacted]@roblox.com)</code>. It came from a single Amazon Web Services (AWS) address. It had read the main page of 18,926 projects, which is practically our entire catalogue, in 26,429 requests over a span of 34 hours.</p><p>After pulling the project pages, it downloaded complete copies of 1,027 files from 712 projects, 145.5 GB in all, before suspending operations during the weekend. The downloads include 364 files (54.8 GB) from 224 projects on SmutBase, which hosts adult content only. With the SFMLab projects marked adult, 345 of the 712 projects (62.6 GB) are adult content.</p><p>The main limiting factor in the number of files downloaded was the crawler's reliance on the free download servers, which have caps on speed limit and number of concurrent downloads. The crawler did not respect our robots.txt, (which it did retrieve), ignored our Terms of Service, (which forbid scraping), and defied the creators' licenses: 423 of the 712 projects are licensed for non-commercial use only. Within the sample it retrieved, it took adult projects at the same rate as everything else. Some examples include models of <a href="https://smutba.se/project/0bc1da85-0f2f-4f5e-b69c-0431707154f8/">Eve from Stellar Blade</a>, <a href="https://smutba.se/project/0f735d5b-1446-46ef-8219-d52639d962d4/">Mei from Overwatch</a>, and a <a href="https://smutba.se/project/01c9a46d-dd73-4706-a861-fb5294a48a84/">Horse cock dildo</a>.</p><h2 id="toc_2">Our response</h2><p>We found it on 18 October and blocked further attempts the same morning. The scraper had suspended its activity by that point, and we did not notice any scraper that weekend. We emailed the address in its user agent on 18 October and asked Roblox for comment. We have not had a reply.</p><p>The crawler resumed scraping activity on October 19th, with all requests getting blocked by our servers. The scraper did not reduce its download attempts when faced with our blocks, and the scrapes seemed to fit neatly within office hours, only briefly pausing for an hour around lunchtime, and ending activity around the end of the workday (CEST). On the 20th, their scraper made its final return at 8:00, slowly tapering requests from lunchtime onward, likely in an effort to test our rate limiting. The requests stopped around 15:00. We sent another email to Roblox for questions, to which they again did not reply.</p><h2 id="toc_3">Speculation</h2><p>This section contains our speculation and assumptions based on public information, and the information we noticed in our logs.</p><p>Roblox has been building a "3D foundation model" since at least 2025.In March 2025 Roblox introduced <a href="https://about.roblox.com/newsroom/2025/03/introducing-roblox-cube">Cube</a>, which it calls its core generative AI system for 3D and 4D. The accompanying <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.15475">paper</a> describes the goal as a foundation model for 3D intelligence. It lists character rigging among the things that model should eventually do, and generating characters and avatars as future work. Its first shape model was trained on roughly 1.5 million 3D assets from licensed and public datasets such as Objaverse, plus free assets from Roblox's own Creator Store whose creators had opted in. In February 2026 Roblox began <a href="https://www.bnnbloomberg.ca/business/2026/02/04/roblox-launches-ai-tech-that-generates-functioning-models-with-natural-language/">rolling out "4D generation"</a> built on Cube.</p><p>The address in the user agent belongs to a researcher who is among the 40-plus authors of that Cube paper. The researcher's public professional profile says they are building a 3D foundation model at Roblox, and their earlier published work is on 3D reconstruction and real-time 3D rendering. We want to be clear: we don't suggest this researcher did anything on their own initiative or out of the ordinary for their job. A data-collection run like this is a company's decision, and the responsibility for it is Roblox's. But the profile says what the user agent says: the people behind this crawler build 3D foundation models.</p><p>The "trellis2" in <code>trellis2-data-sourcing</code> may refer to <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/TRELLIS.2">TRELLIS.2</a>, an open 3D generation model Microsoft released in late 2025. It may mean the data was meant for training or tuning a model like it, or it may just be a project codename. We don't know. What we can say is that TRELLIS.2's published <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/TRELLIS.2/tree/main/data_toolkit">data toolkit</a> contains no web crawler and no user agent, so this crawler and its label were written for the job. It is also possible that Roblox is re-using the Trellis.2 tooling for its own foundational model and it is re-using these scripts for its next model; the tools are open source.</p><p>Our sites are full of things a 3D model-generation system needs and public datasets have relatively little of: complete, textured characters, many of them rigged, in the formats artists actually work in, each with a title, a description and preview images.</p><p>Put together, <strong>we think the most likely explanation is that the crawler was collecting training or evaluation data for Roblox's 3D foundation model work.</strong> We can't prove it, and we don't know whether any of our creators' files ended up in a model.</p><h2 id="toc_4">Roblox's track record</h2><p>This isn't the first time Roblox has been criticised for how it treats the people whose work its platform runs on, or for how it handles other people's rights.</p><ul><li><strong>Young developers.</strong> In 2021 the games outlet People Make Games published a <a href="https://www.nme.com/news/gaming-news/roblox-is-allegedly-exploiting-young-game-developers-3024548">video investigation</a> arguing that Roblox turns its young users into underpaid developers. By its calculation Roblox kept around 75% of the money spent in developers' games, against Steam's 30%, and paid creators in Robux, which they can only cash out once they've earned a minimum amount. Roblox declined to discuss its revenue share and payout minimums. (In September 2025 it <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1315098/000131509825000327/rblx-20250930.htm">raised its cash-out rate</a> by 8.5%.)</li><li><strong>Copyright.</strong> In 2021 the US National Music Publishers' Association sued Roblox for at least $200 million over unlicensed music on the platform. The case <a href="https://www.nmpa.org/nmpa-and-roblox-settle-copyright-suit-launch-music-licensing-talks">settled</a> later that year for an undisclosed amount.</li><li><strong>Child safety.</strong> In October 2024 the short seller Hindenburg Research <a href="https://gameworldobserver.com/2024/10/09/hindenburg-research-roblox-report-inflated-dau-child-safety">accused Roblox</a> of inflating its user numbers and of failing to protect children. Roblox called the report misleading and pointed out that its authors stood to profit from a falling share price. Since then the attorneys general of <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/15/louisiana-attorney-general-sues-roblox">Louisiana</a>, Kentucky, Texas and Florida, among <a href="https://www.nolo.com/legal-encyclopedia/what-are-the-roblox-lawsuits-about.html">other states</a>, have sued Roblox over child safety, and private lawsuits have been consolidated in US federal court. These are allegations, not findings.</li></ul><p>None of this proves anything about the crawler, but it strengthens our opinion that Roblox is a deeply unethical business. It shows a company that has repeatedly been accused of putting growth ahead of the people its platform depends on.</p><h2 id="toc_5">You see how this looks, right?</h2><p>More than a third of Roblox's daily users were <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1315098/000131509825000117/ex992-q12025shareholderl.htm">under 13</a> in 2025, by its own figures. Its rules ban sexual content, and in 2025 it <a href="https://about.roblox.com/newsroom/2025/08/extending-roblox-policy-on-romantic-and-sexual-content">tightened them further</a>.</p><p>Roblox's crawler took 224 projects from a site called SmutBase, which describes itself as exclusively for adult content, and 121 more that are marked adult on SFMLab. It took them at the same rate as everything else: 345 of the 712 projects it downloaded from are adult content. The further scraping attempts did not discriminate between adult content either.</p><p>We want to be careful here. We don't think Roblox set out to collect adult content, and we have no evidence that any of it reached a model or a product. The scrapes were likely automated. But that is exactly the problem: as far as our logs show, nobody checked. A crawler working for a company whose platform tens of millions of children use was pointed at an adult site, and it took explicit models without telling them apart from anything else.</p><p>Models reproduce what they learn from. If adult training data reaches a model that generates assets for Roblox, the only thing between that data and children is whatever filter Roblox builds afterwards, because its data collection didn't filter anything. Furthermore, when a creator deletes a project, or we remove one for breaking our rules or after a copyright notice, it's gone from our sites, but remains in these datasets. One project the crawler took has already been disabled on SFMLab since. If Roblox still has its copy, that copy is still there.</p><p>We're not against adult content. It's our bread and butter. We think consenting adults should be able to access and enjoy this content freely, without the government looking over their shoulder. What we object to is our adult sites being emptied into an exploitative gaming company's training data without anyone asking, and being mixed into content offered to literal children.</p><p><strong>For a company being sued by several US states over child safety, "our AI team bulk-downloaded an adult site without looking" is not a good position to be in.</strong></p><h2 id="toc_6">If the penalty for a crime is a fine, then that law only exists for the lower class.</h2><p><em>This section is opinion.</em></p><p>Roblox isn't the worst offender in AI's appetite for other people's work, just the latest to turn up in our logs. It isn't the only AI company in them, either. In the month before we found <code>trellis2-data-sourcing</code>, Meta's AI crawler, Meta-ExternalAgent, made about 380,000 requests to our sites, almost all from Meta's own network, and loaded 211,000 project pages. We have since blocked their crawler as well.</p><p>The Roblox trellis2 crawler is the first individual crawler we spotted that actually pulled assets uploaded by our users, in flagrant defiance of any copyright or license that might apply to it. It fits a broader context, of AI labs taking content without permission. Here's what the largest companies have been accused of, and in some cases have admitted:</p><ul><li><strong>Meta</strong> downloaded at least 81.7 TB of pirated books by torrent from shadow libraries, including through Anna's Archive, according to <a href="https://www.tweaktown.com/news/103101/meta-accused-of-downloading-torrents-of-81-7tb-of-pirated-books-to-train-its-llama-ai-models/index.html">court filings</a> by the authors suing it. In June 2025 a judge <a href="https://www.authorsalliance.org/2025/06/26/meta-wins-on-fair-use-for-now-but-court-leaves-door-open-for-market-dilution/">ruled</a> that training on the books was fair use <em>on that record</em>, while stressing that the ruling didn't make Meta's conduct lawful in general.</li><li><strong>Meta, again:</strong> the adult studio Strike 3 Holdings <a href="https://torrentfreak.com/copyright-lawsuit-accuses-meta-of-pirating-adult-films-for-ai-training/">alleges</a> that Meta torrented thousands of its films to train AI video models. Meta <a href="https://www.vice.com/en/article/meta-says-the-2400-adult-movies-they-torrented-were-for-personal-use-not-training-ai/">says</a> the downloads were for individuals' "personal use". In June 2026 the court <a href="https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/us-dis-crt-n-d-cal/122823.html">refused to dismiss the case</a>. The irony is hard to miss: Strike 3 is best known for suing thousands of individual BitTorrent users.</li><li><strong>Nvidia</strong> contacted Anna's Archive about high-speed access to its collection of pirated books for training data, and management signed off soon after, according to an <a href="https://www.pcgamer.com/software/ai/nvidia-allegedly-greenlit-the-use-of-pirated-books-from-illegal-sources-to-train-its-ai-models-according-to-an-expanded-class-action-lawsuit/">amended class-action complaint</a> filed in January 2026. Nvidia <a href="https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/artificial-intelligence/nvidia-says-annas-archive-contact-does-not-show-pirated-books-were-used-to-train-its-ai">argued</a> that contacting a pirate site isn't infringement. The court has since <a href="https://www.susmangodfrey.com/wins/susman-godfrey-advances-ai-copyright-case-as-court-denies-nvidia-motion-to-dismiss/">largely refused to dismiss the case</a>.</li><li><strong>Anthropic</strong> downloaded hundreds of thousands of pirated books. A judge found that training on books it had bought was fair use, but keeping a library of pirated copies was not. In July 2026 the court <a href="https://www.jurist.org/news/2026/07/judge-approves-record-1-5-billion-settlement-involving-anthropic/">approved a $1.5 billion settlement</a>, about $3,000 per book.</li></ul><p>Now compare how copyright treats everyone else. In the early 2000s <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capitol_Records,_Inc._v._Thomas-Rasset">Jammie Thomas-Rasset</a> was ordered to pay $222,000 for sharing 24 songs. Internet activist <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aaron_Swartz">Aaron Swartz</a> faced federal charges carrying decades in prison for downloading academic articles. The Internet Archive, a nonprofit library, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hachette_v._Internet_Archive">lost in court</a> for lending scanned copies of books it owned. Meanwhile, the Internet Archive's online library gets repeatedly scanned by AI companies in return.</p><p>We'll be honest about where we stand. We don't like copyright as it exists. We'd like to see it radically reformed, and we wouldn't mourn it if it were abolished. Our community lives in its grey areas every day: many of the models on our sites are fan ports of characters from commercial games, made out of love and shared for free. When a studio sends us a takedown notice, we comply.</p><p>While we as small artists and fan communities get copyright used against us, the world's largest companies are committing copyright infringement at a scale unseen in human history. This has been the case since the invention of the internet of course; digital media make duplication, remixing and resharing trivial, but copyright law never caught up. Filesharing stabbed copyright in the gut. AI is just twisting the knife, and completes the creation an unequal system where copyright infringement is only a crime if you're a low net worth individual.</p><p>So we aren't asking for stricter copyright laws, or stronger protection for copyright holders. That will only benefit the major entertainment companies like Disney and Adobe who have already stacked the deck in their favor, and are happily making deals with Big-Tech and crushing small creators in the process. We're pointing out that the copyright we have isn't applied equally, and is in practice a two tier system. Individuals face ruinous damages for minor infractions. The largest companies in the world face a cost to be negotiated after the fact, if anyone can afford to sue them. A law that only works downwards gives nobody a reason to respect it, and it can't last in this form.</p><p>For time being, we'd like to ask Roblox to respect the norms of this community. You credit people. You respect the license they chose. If you want to use someone's work for something they didn't offer it for, you ask. Those norms cost nothing to follow, and Roblox's crawler followed none of them. This scrape attempt saturated the connections for our free download servers for a couple of days, causing problems for our community of legitimate human users. It shows a lack of respect for the people who make this site what it is. those who produced, shared and host the work that you were about to throw in your AI mixer.</p><h2 id="toc_7">Questions for Roblox</h2><p>We're sad that the emails we sent to Roblox were not met with a timely response, and would thus like to publicly ask Roblox for its response. These are the questions we'd like Roblox to answer:</p><ol><li>Did Roblox run the <code>trellis2-data-sourcing</code> crawler?</li><li>What was the data for? Has any of it been used to train or evaluate a model, and if so, which?</li><li>Why did the crawler ignore our robots.txt rules and our Terms of Service?</li><li>Did anyone review which sites the crawler would visit? Was adult content excluded at any stage?</li><li>Will Roblox delete the files and anything derived from them, and confirm that in writing?</li><li>Will Roblox respect our creators' license choices, NonCommercial in particular, from now on?</li></ol><p>We hope Roblox reaches out to us. Also, if you are a person with information on what Roblox is doing, or a fellow site owner who has been targeted, feel free to get in touch as well. We're eager to hear if there's more of a pattern to the scraper's behavior.</p><p>We've kept the full export of the relevant log files for auditing by external parties if the need arises.</p><h2 id="toc_8">About us</h2><p>Open3DLab, SFMLab and SmutBase are three community sites for free 3D resources, run by a small team. SFMLab started in 2014 for Source Filmmaker models. SmutBase followed in 2016 and is exclusively for adult content. Open3DLab, for general-purpose 3D work, joined in 2020. Creators upload characters, props, maps and environments, choose a license for each project, and anyone can download the files for free. All three sites are intended for adults only.</p><blockquote><p><strong>About the redaction.</strong> The email address in the user agent is a Roblox employee's work address. We've removed the name: this article is about what the Roblox crawler did, not about one person.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p><strong>About Attribution.</strong> We take the user agent at face value. Anyone can type any email address into a user agent, and the crawler ran from Amazon's cloud, not from Roblox's own network. Nothing we've seen suggests impersonation, and the label fits Roblox's published research closely. Still, it is a self-description, and it's the first thing we'd like Roblox to confirm.</p></blockquote></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
