        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Microsoft offers voluntary buyouts to a quarter of its workforce (129 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.kuow.org/technology/2026-10-26/microsoft-offers-voluntary-buyouts-to-a-quarter-of-its-workforce</link>
            <guid>50250937</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:05:15 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.kuow.org/technology/2026-10-26/microsoft-offers-voluntary-buyouts-to-a-quarter-of-its-workforce">https://www.kuow.org/technology/2026-10-26/microsoft-offers-voluntary-buyouts-to-a-quarter-of-its-workforce</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50250937">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p>
        Published&nbsp;October 26, 2026 at 10:16 AM PDT
    </p>
    <meta content="2026-10-26T17:16:19.448Z">


                                        </div><div>
                                        <p>Microsoft is offering voluntary buyouts to about 60,000 employees in an effort to further reduce its headcount.</p><p>Chief People Officer Amy Coleman <a href="https://blogs.microsoft.com/blog/2026/10/26/introducing-a-voluntary-exit-offer/" target="_blank">sent a memo</a> to employees Monday detailing the terms of the voluntary exit offer, which will be available to roughly one quarter of Microsoft’s workforce.</p><p>“This offer gives eligible employees who may be considering a future outside the company a new choice to make that transition now with support,” Hood said in the memo. “It also gives participating organizations another way to adapt their workforce to changing needs.”</p>

<p>The buyouts will be available to select employees in the U.S., U.K., Canada, and India at level 70 and below in the company’s ranking system. That ceiling applies to the vice president level at the company.</p><p>A Microsoft spokesperson said the offer will be comparable to the company’s standard severance package, which varies by market but typically includes base pay for a certain amount of time (based on tenure), stock options granted over time, health care, and career planning services.</p><p>The exit offer comes a few months after Microsoft offered voluntary early retirement to employees of a certain tenure at level 67 and below. About 30% of eligible employees took that offer, though Microsoft expects this broader round of buyouts to have a smaller adoption rate. That’s, in part, because the voluntary retirement program offered more generous terms to incentivize uptake.</p><p>Eligibility for the new program was determined by the heads of each organization at Microsoft. That means the sales organization, some corporate services like marketing, and some engineering teams will not be participating.</p><p>The spokesperson said artificial intelligence is not driving the buyout programs. Microsoft has, however, been on an employee-cutting spree for several years as it invests billions of dollars into the infrastructure needed to train and run AI models.</p><p>“The Voluntary Exit Offer is new for us. … There is no one-size-fits-all approach for every team or every employee,” Hood said in the memo. “These are a few of the ways we’re trying to meet different business needs while continuing to create opportunities for our own people to grow and contribute.”</p>
                                    </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Somebody please stop the spam texts (143 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://notes.npilk.com/stop-the-spam-texts</link>
            <guid>50250016</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:57:32 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://notes.npilk.com/stop-the-spam-texts">https://notes.npilk.com/stop-the-spam-texts</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50250016">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>

    

    

    <p><strong>Summary:</strong> I complain about spam texts and consider why I’m so hesitant to just reply STOP to unsubscribe.</p>

    <p>I moved out of Texas a few years ago. Unfortunately, it doesn’t seem like any statewide midterm candidates have heard about that. In the past month, I’ve gotten dozens of texts like this, usually multiple per day:</p>

    <div>
    <p><img src="https://notes.npilk.com/resources/stop-the-spam-texts/texts.jpeg" alt="Three political text messages: a voting reminder from OurBallot, a Don Huffines campaign ad, and a Clear Insights survey request"></p><p><em>Some of the texts I received while working on this post</em></p>
    </div>

    <p>They seem to be mostly from Republicans, but I’ve gotten a little bit of everything — texts from Democrats, independents, PACs, unbiased pollsters, obviously biased pollsters, even those weird <a href="https://www.fox4news.com/news/political-mailers-transgender-resources-children-north-texas">false flag texts</a> that went around. I don’t remember signing up for anything, and certainly would never have intended to, but clearly my number got on a list somehow.</p>

    <p>This is pretty annoying, because text is my highest-signal communication channel. Most of what’s in there is from family and friends, plus the occasional 2FA code. The little red notification bubble means a lot more on Messages than other apps — which of course is exactly why politicians, marketers, and scammers want so badly to get in there.</p>

    <p>It’s not just the elections, either — this has been getting noticeably worse over time. This year I’ve started to get a ton of these types of messages, sometimes as many as one a day:</p>

    <p><img src="https://notes.npilk.com/resources/stop-the-spam-texts/text2.jpeg" alt="Text message from 'Amy from TN Tree Service' offering a free estimate for tree trimming">
    </p>

    <p>It turns out, despite seeming all personal and handwritten, that if you reply STOP, these messages also reveal themselves to be part of a larger campaign. (Shouldn’t this be illegal, by the way?)</p>

    <p><img src="https://notes.npilk.com/resources/stop-the-spam-texts/text_stop.jpeg" alt="Replying STOP to the tree service text returns an automated opt-out confirmation">
    </p>

    <p>So it seems like the solution is to reply STOP to all of these and eventually you won’t get as many, right?</p>

    <p>Except I haven’t done that. I don’t want to do that. Except for that “experiment” above, I have instead chosen “Delete and Report Spam” for every single one of these texts… even though it doesn’t seem to do anything.</p>

    <p>What’s crazy to me is that Apple has actually added a Spam folder to Messages, and there are a few things in there. But even though I’ve been reporting all this stuff as spam for months, if not longer, none of the new messages I get seem to get filtered into the Spam folder.</p>

    <p>So again, why not just reply STOP to these messages? Why am I so averse to doing that?</p>

    <p>After some reflection, I think I figured it out: <strong>replying with STOP feels like I’m accepting this vision of the world.</strong></p>

    <p>If I reply STOP, I’m basically saying, “Okay, you’re allowed to send me whatever you want, and then I have to send back STOP for you to knock it off.” And I want to fundamentally reject that framing. Mass-texting and spam campaigns shouldn’t get that right by default, and the burden shouldn’t be on me to individually opt-out of every single one of these campaigns.</p>

    <p>So if you’re reading this and you work on iOS at Apple, <em>please help</em>. This feels like a great use case for all this new Apple Intelligence (similar to the <a href="https://notes.npilk.com/experiments-with-ai-adblock">AI adblock experiment</a> I ran a while back). If I report something as Spam, maybe an option could pop up to “Filter messages like this to Spam?” or something, and then the unwanted text gets added to a list of examples. So then next time I get a text from an unknown number, the LLM reads the list of spam examples, and if the new message is similar, it goes to Spam. (That’s just off the top of my head; I’m sure there are even better ideas to combat this.)</p>

    <p>Recently, I did find a “Delete and Stop” option, which auto-sends the STOP message for me. But it seems like “Delete and Stop” and “Delete and Report Spam” are mutually exclusive. You’d think that reporting as spam would also auto-send a STOP message, but the number of texts I’ve gotten from Don Huffines despite reporting each one suggests otherwise.</p>

    <p>Hopefully things will calm down after the election. But for goodness’ sake, somebody please do something about this. I was going to say we fixed this problem for email, but I’m not really sure we did — these days I get tons of marketing messages despite always trying to opt out on every single website whenever I purchase something. And I get multiple spam calls a day too, even though I’m on the Do Not Call registry. So maybe we’re just doomed to be eternally pestered by whoever wants our money or our vote. Here’s hoping they don’t figure out how to get into my Slack. <span>⧈</span></p>

    <hr>

    

    </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[The EPA Is Quietly Killing Its Web Archive (295 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/10/epa-online-archive-deletion-zeldin-camp-lejeune-trump-clean-power/</link>
            <guid>50249275</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:05:41 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/10/epa-online-archive-deletion-zeldin-camp-lejeune-trump-clean-power/">https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/10/epa-online-archive-deletion-zeldin-camp-lejeune-trump-clean-power/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50249275">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><section id="post-1244339">

			<article itemprop="articleBody">
				
				

				
<div>
	<p><img width="990" height="557" src="https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?w=990" alt="Blue and white website that says, &quot;EPA: United States Environmental Protection Agency&quot; in the center. At the top, the website says &quot;An official website of the United States government.&quot; At the bottom, the website says &quot;Environmental Topics.&quot; The website has a slight glare." decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png 2000w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=208,117 208w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=321,181 321w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=630,354 630w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=990,557 990w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=1536,864 1536w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=50,28 50w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=1300,731 1300w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=642,361 642w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2200850898-fotor-20261023844.png?resize=768,432 768w" sizes="(max-width: 709px) 85vw, (max-width: 909px) 67vw, (max-width: 1362px) 62vw, 840px"></p><p><span>Screenshot of the Environmental Protection Agency's homepage.</span><span>Mario Guti/Getty</span></p></div>

				<div><p>Get your news from a source that’s not owned and controlled by oligarchs. <a href="https://www.motherjones.com/newsletters/?mj_oac=Article_Top_No_Oligarchs" data-ga-category="TopOfArticle" data-ga-label="NewsletterPromoCovid" data-ga-action="click|https://www.motherjones.com/newsletters/?mj_oac=Article_Top_Support">Sign up for the free <em>Mother Jones Daily</em>.</a></p></div>




<p><span>In a little-noticed</span> webpage update, the Environmental Protection Agency has <a href="https://archive.epa.gov/">announced</a> a plan to decommission its online web archive at the end of the year, a move that would wipe tens of thousands of publicly available environmental resources from the web. The discreet banner notice comes in stark contrast to EPA Administrator Lee Zeldin’s recent public takedowns of the agency’s <a href="https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2026/09/the-epa-would-rather-monitor-water-for-abortion-pills-than-pollution/">power plant pollution standards</a> and <a href="https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/10/epa-zeldin-methane-emissions-rules-rollback-oil-gas-super-emitter-program/">federal methane regulatory system</a>.</p>



<p>“The EPA’s online archive makes information about a wide variety of environmental issues and past policies available to the public, dating back at least 20 years,” said Gretchen Gehrke, co-founder of the Environmental Data and Governance Initiative (EDGI). “This information is irreplaceable, and if the archive is decommissioned, that information will be lost forever.”</p>



<p>For decades, the web archive has been an unparalleled resource for researchers, legislators, policymakers, and the public writ large. It houses everything from fact sheets and environmental reports to agency policy changes and regulatory actions, making it a tool for “<a href="https://blog.ucs.org/science-blogger/the-epa-is-shuttering-its-online-archive-why-that-matters/">democratic oversight</a>,” according to Gehrke, who for <a href="https://envirodatagov.org/about/">ten years</a> has been tracking changes to vulnerable federal environmental data.</p>



<figure><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="650" height="366" src="https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Screenshot-2026-10-22-at-12.40.08-PM-fotor-202610238731.png" alt="A webpage that says, &quot;EPA Archive - Home.&quot; Below the title, the webpage says, &quot;Content in EPA's Web Archive is no longer being updated and links may not function; however, the materials in this archive may be useful as background documents to supplement current information or to provide a historical perspective on a topic.&quot; Below that, the webpage says, &quot;EPA Archive Retiring - The EPA Archive will retire on December 31, 2026. Please search the EPA snapshot collections for historical information or the main EPA website for the most up-to-date information.&quot; " srcset="https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Screenshot-2026-10-22-at-12.40.08-PM-fotor-202610238731.png 650w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Screenshot-2026-10-22-at-12.40.08-PM-fotor-202610238731.png?resize=208,117 208w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Screenshot-2026-10-22-at-12.40.08-PM-fotor-202610238731.png?resize=321,181 321w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Screenshot-2026-10-22-at-12.40.08-PM-fotor-202610238731.png?resize=630,354 630w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Screenshot-2026-10-22-at-12.40.08-PM-fotor-202610238731.png?resize=50,28 50w, https://www.motherjones.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Screenshot-2026-10-22-at-12.40.08-PM-fotor-202610238731.png?resize=642,361 642w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 709px) 85vw, (max-width: 909px) 67vw, (max-width: 984px) 61vw, (max-width: 1362px) 45vw, 600px"><figcaption><span>Screenshot of the EPA’s online archive webpage.</span><span>Rachel Santarsiero</span></figcaption></figure>



<p>This isn’t the first time the environmental agency has tried to shutter the online web archive. In <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20220124192618/https://archive.epa.gov/">late January 2022</a>, the Biden administration EPA announced plans to discontinue the online repository, citing burdensome financial upkeep; through a Freedom of Information Act request, Gehrke was able to determine that the agency spent roughly $2 million a year to maintain it. A coalition of environmentalists, historians, and librarians swiftly condemned the decision, with groups like the EDGI and American Society for Environmental History calling the announcement “<a href="https://envirodatagov.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/EPA-online-archive-letter.pdf">alarming</a>” and cause for a “<a href="https://cdn.wildapricot.com/295306/resources/Documents/Letter%20to%20EPA%20formatted%20final.pdf?version=1654901178000&amp;&amp;Signature=GwFFKB0KP3eyRkMt4T3ly8XC2kDqvfdrlFh7SuLFUr92RhnXkhKG7pd7By9TWEHAVrNOXROi79fecEMuhR42DbI0XIZZ3o7xLnzkFbHp-tHaxJTpCEA0GQwSdl9U9LutD121ohqvwvs59yOC7sc-Ov0IjTxoAm20BatQVEN000sNb21g0TjSBr~u9CY7y5srdD5yq9P4n9Q1JvCwqAhdVpjdCb-z1fYPBc4Q0ks2eThrjkUPAjRUSi0Onw7lLYK9mgWmmjgei4j2L1HUfzzJeKD4snkVQ45meNgYc79CCkyEa9Mya7ssLjvzZ1loc23jNIVK0BgdJT~138wFwyY-RQ__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=K27MGQSHTHAGGF">tremendous gap</a>” in the historical record.&nbsp;</p>



<p>After five months of public backlash, the EPA acquiesced, in part by announcing it would delay the web archive’s retirement by one year to <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20220719113908/https://archive.epa.gov/">July 2023</a>. I reported on it at the time for <a href="https://www.newsecuritybeat.org/2022/07/delaying-inevitable-uncertain-future-epas-online-archive/"><em>New Security Beat</em></a>:&nbsp;</p>



<blockquote>
<p>“News of this welcome reprieve for the EPA digital archive raises a significant question: Why continue with plans to shut it down at all? Postponement does not resolve the eventual damage to government transparency and historical record keeping that the archive’s demise will create. And the concern of organizations opposed to closure will not be satisfied by an additional year of operation.”&nbsp;</p>
</blockquote>



<p>In <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20230309120345/https://archive.epa.gov/">March 2023</a>, the agency walked back its decision further still by adjusting the banner announcement to say that the online web archive was simply “retiring”—end date not included. Since then, over the last three and a half years, the online resource has soldiered on quietly, no longer being updated, but still accessible to the public. <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260902072615/https://archive.epa.gov/">Until a few weeks ago</a>, when the EPA updated the archive’s expiration date to December 31, 2026.&nbsp;</p>



<p>While the usability of the archive has historically been far from perfect—researchers often encountered dead links and frustratingly limited search capabilities—the archive has held a trove of tens of thousands of EPA documents dating back to the 1990s simply not available elsewhere.</p>



<p>Major political environmental inflection points could be tracked through the archive, such as President George W. Bush’s <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20220119152748/https:/archive.epa.gov/clearskies/web/html/">2003 Clear Skies Initiative</a>, or President Barack Obama’s <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20220604105927/https:/archive.epa.gov/epa/cleanpowerplan/clean-power-plan-existing-power-plants-regulatory-actions.html">2015 Clean Power Plan</a>—two initiatives that sought to dramatically transform power plant pollution standards. And during President Trump’s first term, when the administration <a href="https://blog.ucs.org/science-blogger/censorship-of-federal-environmental-agency-websites-under-trump-what-we-learned-and-how-to-protect-public-information-moving-forward/?_gl=1*19olbv2*_gcl_au*MTg4NTY4NjY4MC4xNzkxMzk1MTI1*_ga*NTg2ODY1NjQzLjE3OTEzOTUxMjU.*_ga_VB9DKE4V36*czE3OTI2ODc3NjAkbzMkZzAkdDE3OTI2ODc3NjAkajYwJGwwJGgxNzgyNjc1ODU4JGQ3ME9UMUQ0QS1hM0xrNVI3RjlHWmwwTUQwYVgxalJ3ZW53">deleted</a> the majority of the EPA’s climate change web resources, the online resource crucially maintained and made accessible these defunct pages for the public.&nbsp;</p>



<p>The archival repository has also been a tool for environmental enforcement and oversight. In 1989, after an <a href="https://cumulis.epa.gov/supercpad/SiteProfiles/index.cfm?fuseaction=second.Cleanup&amp;id=0403185#bkground">investigation</a> found that military operations had contaminated the soil, sediment, surface water, and groundwater at Marine Corps Base Camp Lejeune and the Marine Corps Air Station in North Carolina, the EPA listed the base on its National Priorities List. Documents on the EPA’s decades-long investigation were preserved on the archive and helped nearby residents and service members on base overcome doubts about the agency’s effectiveness and better evaluate chemical exposures. <a href="https://www.military.com/trump-says-no-money-going-towards-camp-lejeune-toxic-water-claims-if-gop-loses-midterm-elections">Earlier this week</a>, President Trump said he would withhold compensation due to service members and their families exposed at Camp Lejeune if Republicans lose their majority in Congress.</p>



<p>Now, the EPA’s latest move could further undermine public confidence at a time when trust in government is near <a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2025/12/04/public-trust-in-government-1958-2025/">historic lows</a>.&nbsp;</p>



<p>“The EPA’s insistence on decommissioning its web archive will only continue to erode public trust—at the exact time the public and lawmakers need agency information the most,” Lauren Harper, the first Daniel Ellsberg chair on government secrecy for the Freedom of the Press Foundation, wrote in an email. “Killing easy access to records forces researchers and lawmakers to flood an already underwater FOIA office just to see once-public records.”</p>



<p>The recently updated webpage states that historical information can still be found in the EPA’s “<a href="https://www.epa.gov/utilities/wwwepagov-snapshots">snapshot collections</a>,” but given the second Trump administration’s <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2026/aug/18/trump-federal-data-deleted-altered">crusade against public data</a> and <a href="https://www.npr.org/2025/05/02/nx-s1-5385272/epa-environmental-protection-agency-cuts-trump-zeldin">evisceration of funds</a> to federal programs and personnel, it’s hard to know for sure if all, or even a fraction, of the archive’s contents will live on.&nbsp;</p>



<p>“EPA should expand and systematize the archive, not throw it away and expect that three discrete snapshots will suffice,” said Gehrke.&nbsp;</p>



<p>The EPA did not respond to a request for comment.&nbsp;</p>
			</article>

			

		</section></div>]]></description>
