            <pubDate>Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:35:12 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.seattletimes.com/business/microsoft/microsoft-offers-mass-buyouts-for-roughly-a-quarter-of-its-workforce/">https://www.seattletimes.com/business/microsoft/microsoft-offers-mass-buyouts-for-roughly-a-quarter-of-its-workforce/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50245524">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><article id="post-20074099">
          
<header>

  
  

  
    
  
  <div>
    <p><time datetime="2026-10-26 08:30:00"> Oct. 26, 2026 at 8:30 am </time><time datetime=" 1793003402 "> Updated Oct. 26, 2026 at 8:30 am </time>    </p>
    
  </div>
      
  </header>
          

  

    


<div>

  <div>
        
        <p><span>Seattle Times business reporter</span></p>    </div>

  
  <div id="article-content">
    <p>Microsoft is offering buyouts to about 60,000 employees, or roughly a quarter of its workforce, as the company tries to control its head count while avoiding layoffs.</p><p>The Redmond-based tech giant announced Monday morning that eligible employees at the vice president level and below in the U.S., Canada, India and the United Kingdom would receive a “voluntary exit offer” of a standard severance package, based on their location.</p><p>In a memo to employees, Microsoft Chief People Officer Amy Coleman said the move was building off the <a href="https://www.seattletimes.com/business/microsoft/microsoft-offers-buyouts-for-longtime-employees/">voluntary retirement program earlier this year</a>, which was available to more than 8,700 U.S.-based employees, roughly one-third of whom took the offer.</p><p>Since May 2025, Microsoft has been trying to reduce its corporate workforce, first through waves of mass layoffs and now through more creative buyout programs. The company laid off about 15,000 employees last year over the course of several months, affecting about 3,200 Washington-based workers.</p><p>Layoffs have continued this year; they’ve been smaller, however, and have <a href="https://www.seattletimes.com/business/microsoft/microsoft-cuts-4800-across-xbox-sales-divisions-redeploys-hundreds-more/" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.seattletimes.com/business/microsoft/microsoft-cuts-4800-across-xbox-sales-divisions-redeploys-hundreds-more/">mostly affected the company’s gaming division</a>, which is under new leadership and in the middle of a hard reset.</p><p><em>This story will be updated.</em></p><p>Microsoft Philanthropies underwrites some Seattle Times journalism projects.</p>    
        <div>
   <p><span>Alex Halverson</span>:       <span>206-652-6352</span> or <span><a href="mailto:ahalverson@seattletimes.com">ahalverson@seattletimes.com</a></span>. <span>Alex Halverson is a tech reporter at The Seattle Times, where he covers some of the region’s largest employers, including Amazon and Microsoft. </span>   </p>
